<ProjectLayout
  title="Private networking across environments"
  description="WireGuard tunnels and corporate VPN connecting distinct clusters and environments, with routing, MTU, and cloud firewall-layer diagnostics."
  domain="network"
  caseId="03"
>
  <h2>Context</h2>
  <p>
    Multiple environments (production, staging/review) needing to communicate securely with each
    other and with a third-party corporate VPN, without publicly exposing internal services. The
    network surface involved Kubernetes clusters, database VMs, and legacy services — each with its
    own set of firewall rules.
  </p>

  <h2>Approach</h2>
  <p>
    Two dedicated <strong>WireGuard</strong> tunnels connecting distinct environments, complemented by
    integration with an external partner's corporate VPN client (FortiClient). To reduce human error when
    switching context between environments, I automated VPN and{' '}
    <code>kubectl</code> context switching into a single script.
  </p>

  <h2>Recurring diagnosis</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Routing gaps</strong> — missing routes in WireGuard's <code>AllowedIPs</code>, or
      routing tables with <code>Table = off</code> requiring manual routes via{' '}
      <code>PostUp</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Firewall layers in OCI</strong> — Security Lists (subnet-level) and NSGs (resource-level)
      overlapping in non-obvious ways; a block could be in either layer, requiring systematic checking
      of both.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>MTU</strong> — packets silently fragmenting on tunnels with misconfigured MTU, causing intermittent
      timeouts that were hard to correlate with the real cause without traffic capture.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>DNS-over-HTTPS</strong> — browsers ignoring the VPN's internal resolver for private domains,
      causing name resolution to fail even with the correct network route.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Result</h2>
  <p>
    Stable connectivity between environments with automated context switching, reducing network
    troubleshooting time — most cases today are resolved by checking a known-causes checklist before
    any deeper investigation.
  </p>
</ProjectLayout>
